  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nOur late-season heat wave is underway and looks to continue\nthrough the end of the week. The only major change to the\nforecast is to extend the ongoing Heat Advisory for the entire\nregion through 7 PM CDT Saturday. Ensemble guidance continues to\ntrend slower with breaking down the very stout H5 ridge over the\nregion, so heat index values at Heat Advisory criteria will \ncontinue an extra day. \n\nWe again want to emphasize that while the air temperatures \nforecast are not unheard of for the latter half of September, \nthe humidity and heat index values are very anomalous. Our \nconcern for heat- related health impacts is high due to the \nnumerous outdoor fall season events that will be impacted by \nthis heat wave. We urge anyone planning to participate in \nprolonged outdoor activities to follow heat safety guidelines \nwhenever possible or consider delaying them until the cooler \nportions of the day in the early morning or evening hours. \n\nEnsemble guidance continues to show H5 heights around 596-600 \ndm and 850mb temperatures peaking around 19-22C each afternoon \nthrough at least Friday. Both values are near the climatological\nmaximum values for the time of year. This will yield maximum \ntemperatures in the upper 90s to lower 100s and minimum \ntemperatures in the lower to middle 70s during this time. Both \nthe forecast high and low temperatures will approach or break \nrecord hot/warm values this week. See the Climate section below \nfor more details. \n\nDew point temperatures will reach the upper 60s to lower 70s \ntoday and Thursday. Combined with the heat, peak heat index \nvalues will reach 103-109. On Friday, dew point temperatures \nwill draw down into the middle to upper 60s, yielding slightly \nlower heat index values. On Saturday, dew point temperatures \nwill draw down further into the lower to middle 60s. However, \nwith air temperatures in the upper 90s to lower 100s, peak heat \nindex values will still climb over 100, which is why the Heat \nAdvisory has been extended through Saturday evening.\n\nWe do have small PoPs in the forecast today and Thursday, mainly\nnorth of I-64. This is due to potential forcing from remnant\noutflow boundaries from convection passing to the north.\nHowever, these storms will not provide much relief from the\nheat. \n\nThe H5 ridge will finally begin to break down early next week, \nbut high temperatures will still reach the lower to middle 90s \non Sunday. However, there is better model agreement that some \nnoticeable relief from the heat will arrive Monday into Tuesday\nas the ridge retreats to the west and a frontal boundary \nattempts to move south through the area. This will yield high \ntemperatures in the upper 80s to lower 90s on Monday and middle \nto upper 80s on Tuesday. Additionally, there may be enough \nmoisture and forcing from the cold front to kick off isolated to\nwidely scattered showers and thunderstorms on both days.",
